// @run
// @log PRESENT
// @log false
// @log -1
// @log 7
// @log absent
// @log absent
// The annotations below are redundant to INFERENCE, and that is worth seeing rather
// than hiding: the checker already knows `miss` is `i32 | null`, so the defect is
// purely emit-side. They are written out anyway because the annotated spelling is the
// one this file is about.
// @hint redundant type annotation: `miss` is inferred as `i32 | null`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `hit` is inferred as `i32 | null`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `smiss` is inferred as `string | null`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `pmiss` is inferred as `P | null`
// MISCOMPILE xfail. A MISS on a NUMERIC-valued map, read as `V | null` and narrowed
// with `is V`, answers **PRESENT** — a silent wrong answer, `vl check`-clean, in the
// spelling the compiler's OWN diagnostic recommends:
//
//     print of a union value (i32 | null) is type-valid but not yet supported by
//     codegen — narrow it first, e.g. `if v is i32 { print(v) }`
//
// The first `@log PRESENT` below is the BUG, pinned so it is tracked and so the fix
// fails this file loudly. It should read `absent`.
//
// THE CAUSE, from `emitMapGet`'s own comment: "A miss yields the rep's empty value:
// 0.0 for an f64 map, 0 for an i64 map". For a scalar value type there is NO BOX —
// the read hands back a bare scalar, so the `| null` in the type has no runtime
// representation at all and `is V` is trivially true. `emitMapGetUnionBox` (the path
// that DOES synthesize a null-tagged box on a miss) is only reached for a
// union-valued map.
//
// IT FOLLOWS THE REP SPLIT, which is what bounds it. Correct today: `boolean` (an i32
// sentinel), `string` (a nullable ref) and a STRUCT value (also a ref) — all three
// carry a representable absent. Wrong: `i32`, `i64`, `f64`, `f32`, i.e. exactly the
// widths that would need the box.
//
// THE OTHER TWO READS ARE CORRECT, which is what makes this a narrowing defect rather
// than a map defect: `.has(k)` probes the entry table and answers `false`, and
// `m[k] ?? d` lowers through `emitMapGetOr`, which also probes. Only the narrowing
// spelling lies. A HIT is correct too, so nothing is wrong with the value path.
//
// WHY THE FIX IS NOT MECHANICAL. Making this correct means routing a scalar-valued
// map read through the union-box path whenever it is consumed as a nullable — which
// ALLOCATES on every such read, on the hot path of the exact SoA/rule-table code the
// map tier exists for. That is a correctness-versus-cost ruling, not a patch.
//
// FOUND while fixing the adjacent gap that `const v = m[k]` (INFERRED, no annotation)
// could not be narrowed at all — a loud `is test but no union type declared`. That
// fix worked for every value type but was REVERTED on finding this: it would have
// spread the silent wrong answer from one spelling to two, and a loud error is worth
// more than a quiet lie until this is ruled on.

const m: {[string]: i32} = Map()
m["a"] = 7

// THE BUG. A key that was never set narrows as present.
const miss: i32 | null = m["nope"]
if miss is i32 {
  print("PRESENT") // should be `absent`
} else {
  print("absent")
}

// The two reads that get it right, as the inverted control.
print(m.has("nope")) // false
print(m["nope"] ?? -1) // -1

// A hit is correct — the value path is fine.
const hit: i32 | null = m["a"]
if hit is i32 {
  print(hit) // 7
} else {
  print("absent")
}

// The rep split: a STRING value carries a representable absent and answers correctly.
const ms: {[string]: string} = Map()
ms["a"] = "x"
const smiss: string | null = ms["nope"]
if smiss is string {
  print(smiss) // absent
} else {
  print("absent")
}

// So does a STRUCT value.
type P = { x: i32 }
const mp: {[string]: P} = Map()
mp["a"] = { x: 1 }
const pmiss: P | null = mp["nope"]
if pmiss is P {
  print("PRESENT") // absent
} else {
  print("absent")
}
